---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/okad-house.css';
---

<Base title="UGC for Food & Beverage Brands | OKAD Agency" description="UGC video content for food, beverage, and CPG brands. Recipe integration, taste testing, lifestyle content. Creators who make food look real.">
  <div class="okh">

    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="khero" style="padding:80px 0 48px">
      <div class="w" style="max-width:900px">
        <span class="kick">UGC for Food & Beverage Brands</span>
        <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);margin-bottom:16px">Food UGC That Makes People <span class="cap">Stop Scrolling and Start Craving</span></h1>
        <p class="lead">Food is the most visual, most sensory category in advertising. Nothing triggers desire like watching a real person crack into a crispy shell, pour a golden sauce over fresh pasta, or take that first sip of something cold on a hot day. When real people cook, taste, and react on camera, it creates a visceral pull that polished studio photography simply cannot replicate. That is why UGC consistently outperforms professional food photography in paid social ads — it feels real because it is real, and audiences respond with clicks, saves, and purchases.</p>
        <div class="row" style="margin-top:24px">
          <a class="btn" href="https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a call</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="mailto:olga@okad.agency">Email us</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY UGC FOR FOOD -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:8px">Why <span class="cap g">UGC</span> Works for Food Brands</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px;max-width:64ch">Food is uniquely suited to user-generated content. It is sensory, emotional, and deeply shareable. People do not just eat — they photograph, film, review, and recommend. That natural behavior is the exact engine that makes UGC the most effective content format for food and beverage brands running paid social. Unlike fashion or tech, food content triggers an immediate physical response: hunger. Harness that, and your ad does half the work before the viewer even reads your copy.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(260px,1fr));gap:16px">

        <div style="padding:24px;background:#f4f9e8;border-radius:12px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Appetite Appeal</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Real food in real kitchens triggers cravings more effectively than studio photography. When a viewer sees a home cook pulling apart a grilled cheese with strings of melted cheddar in their own kitchen, the mental distance between them and that experience shrinks to almost nothing. Studio shots look aspirational but distant. UGC looks achievable and immediate, which is exactly what drives someone to add a product to their cart. The imperfect lighting, the slightly messy counter, the steam rising from a pot — these details signal authenticity and make the food feel accessible rather than staged.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:#f3e5f5;border-radius:12px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Authentic Reactions</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Genuine taste-test moments build trust and curiosity in ways that scripted testimonials never can. When a creator takes a first bite and their eyes widen, or they pause mid-chew to say "wait, this is actually really good" — that micro-moment of surprise is more persuasive than any headline. Viewers have become experts at spotting fake enthusiasm. A real reaction, even an understated one, carries more weight than a rehearsed endorsement. These moments also generate high engagement because people want to comment, share their own opinions, and debate flavor comparisons.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:#e1f5fe;border-radius:12px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Recipe Integration</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Showing your product as an ingredient in a recipe is the most natural, non-salesy form of product placement in advertising. When a creator uses your hot sauce in a marinade, your oat milk in their morning smoothie, or your pasta sauce in a weeknight dinner, the product becomes part of a useful piece of content — not an interruption. Recipe content has among the highest save rates on Instagram and TikTok because people bookmark it for later, which means your brand stays in their saved folder for weeks. This format also naturally demonstrates product usage, quantity, and versatility without feeling like a tutorial.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:#f6f5f4;border-radius:12px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Shareability</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Food content is the most shared category on social media, consistently outperforming travel, fashion, and fitness in share-to-view ratios. People tag friends in food videos ("we need to try this"), send recipes to partners, and share taste-test reactions in group chats. This organic amplification means your paid UGC often earns significant free distribution. A single well-made food UGC video can generate shares that effectively lower your cost per impression by 30-50% compared to non-food categories. The combination of visual appeal, practical value, and emotional resonance makes food content inherently viral in a way that most product categories can only envy.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CONTENT TYPES -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:8px">Content <span class="cap y">Formats</span> We Produce</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px;max-width:64ch">Each format serves a different stage of the buyer journey — from discovery to craving to purchase. We match the format to your objective and platform.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:16px">

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Taste Test & First Reaction</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">The creator tries your product for the first time on camera — a blind taste test, an unboxing with immediate tasting, or a "my honest review" format. The power of this format lies in the genuine surprise moment. When someone who has never tried your snack, drink, or sauce reacts with real enthusiasm, it is more convincing than any celebrity endorsement. We brief creators to be honest, which paradoxically makes the positive reactions land harder. This format works exceptionally well for new product launches, flavor extensions, and brands entering new markets where awareness is low but product quality is high. The best taste test videos capture the full arc: anticipation before the first bite, the moment of tasting, and the immediate verbal reaction — all within 15-30 seconds that hold attention from start to finish.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Recipe Integration</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">A creator cooks a full recipe using your product as a key ingredient — quick weeknight dinners, meal prep ideas, breakfast routines, or party appetizers. The product appears naturally throughout the cooking process, from grabbing it off the shelf to the final plated dish. Recipe content has the highest save rate of any food format on Instagram (averaging 8-12% save rates vs. 2-3% for standard posts), making it a long-term asset in saved collections. We develop recipes that are simple enough to follow, visually compelling, and designed to showcase what makes your product different — whether that is flavor, convenience, health benefits, or versatility. Each recipe video gets 3-5 hook variants for testing.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Grocery Haul</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">The creator films themselves finding your product in-store, explains why they chose it over competitors, and shows the purchase in context with their other grocery picks. This format is gold for retail distribution because it drives in-store foot traffic and shelf awareness. When a viewer sees someone pick up your product at a Whole Foods, Target, or local grocery store, it answers the unspoken question "where do I find this?" without feeling like an ad. Grocery haul content also benefits from the "recommendation from a friend" dynamic — viewers trust fellow shoppers more than brand messaging. This format works especially well for products with strong packaging, competitive shelf positioning, or clean-label differentiators that are visible on the package.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Morning / Evening Routine</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Your product becomes part of a creator's daily ritual — the coffee they reach for first thing, the protein bar they grab before the gym, the evening tea that winds down their day, or the snack they prep for their kids' lunchboxes. Routine content positions your product as a daily habit rather than a one-time purchase, which directly supports subscription models and repeat buying behavior. These videos perform well because they tap into the "day in my life" trend that dominates TikTok and Instagram Reels, giving your product organic exposure within content people are already seeking out. The lifestyle context also helps viewers self-identify: "that looks like my morning" becomes "I should try that product."</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Brand Comparison</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">An honest side-by-side comparison of your product versus a well-known competitor — taste, texture, ingredients, price, and overall impression. This format directly intercepts purchase-intent searches and captures audiences who are already in the consideration phase. Comparison content consistently generates high engagement because it invites debate in the comments, which the algorithm rewards with increased distribution. We position these as genuine, balanced reviews where your product wins on specific merits rather than blanket superiority — audiences see through one-sided comparisons instantly. When done right, a comparison video can convert competitor customers and establish your brand as the premium, healthier, or better-value alternative in your category.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Seasonal & Holiday Content</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Holiday recipes, summer drink ideas, game day snack spreads, back-to-school lunchbox packing, Valentine's Day desserts, or Thanksgiving side dishes featuring your product. Seasonal content has a natural urgency built in — viewers know the moment will pass, which drives faster action. This format also lets you tap into high-volume seasonal search trends and hashtags that can multiply your reach significantly. We plan seasonal content 4-6 weeks ahead of each moment so your creative is ready to launch when interest peaks, not when it is already declining. Seasonal UGC can be repurposed year after year with minor updates, making it one of the highest-ROI content investments for food brands with products that have year-round availability. The key moments we plan around include Super Bowl snack season, Valentine's Day, summer grilling and drinks, back-to-school, Halloween treats, Thanksgiving, and holiday gifting — each with its own set of hooks, recipe angles, and emotional triggers that align with what audiences are actively searching for during those windows.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY FOOD BRANDS ARE SHIFTING TO UGC -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:16px">Why Food Brands Are <span class="cap p">Shifting</span> to UGC</h2>

      <div style="display:grid;gap:20px;max-width:64ch">
        <p style="font-size:15px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.7;margin:0">The era of polished food photography dominating ad performance is over. Between 2021 and 2025, Meta and TikTok ad data consistently showed that UGC-style creative outperformed studio-produced food content by 30-50% on click-through rate and 20-35% on cost per acquisition. The reason is simple: platforms reward content that keeps people watching, and people watch content that looks like it belongs in their feed. A beautifully lit product shot on a marble countertop might earn a like, but a video of someone genuinely enjoying that same product in their apartment kitchen earns a click, a share, and a purchase.</p>

        <p style="font-size:15px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.7;margin:0">TikTok and Instagram Reels fundamentally changed what "good food content" looks like. Before short-form video, food marketing was dominated by the overhead shot, the slow-motion pour, and the pristine plating. That aesthetic still has its place in editorial, but in the feed, it reads as advertising — and advertising gets skipped. The new standard is vertical video, handheld, shot in a real kitchen, with a real person talking to the camera. The production value is in the storytelling and the hook, not in the lighting rig. Brands that adapted early — like Liquid Death, Siete Foods, and Chomps — built massive audiences and eight-figure revenue on the back of creator content that cost a fraction of traditional production.</p>

        <p style="font-size:15px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.7;margin:0">The economics make the shift even more compelling. A single professional food photography shoot — with a food stylist, prop stylist, photographer, studio rental, and post-production — typically costs $5,000-$15,000 and yields 10-20 static images. For the same budget, a food brand can produce 30-50 UGC videos with multiple creators, multiple formats, and multiple hooks to test. Each video is a unique creative variation that feeds the algorithm's need for fresh content. With Meta and TikTok now recommending brands refresh their ad creative every 2-3 weeks to avoid fatigue, the volume advantage of UGC is not just nice to have — it is a competitive necessity.</p>

        <p style="font-size:15px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.7;margin:0">There is also the viral potential. Authentic food reactions are among the most shared content types on social media. A genuine moment of surprise — the crunch of a chip, the stretch of melted cheese, the unexpected flavor combination that actually works — can turn a paid ad into organic content that earns millions of impressions for free. In 2024 and 2025, several DTC food brands reported that their best-performing paid ads went semi-viral organically, effectively reducing their blended CPA by 40-60%. That kind of earned media multiplier simply does not happen with studio food photography. The content has to feel real for people to want to share it, and UGC is real by definition.</p>

        <p style="font-size:15px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.7;margin:0">The creator economy has also matured in food specifically. Five years ago, finding creators who could cook on camera, speak naturally, and deliver footage that met basic quality standards was a genuine challenge. Today, there is a deep bench of food-focused UGC creators who understand lighting, pacing, vertical framing, and how to make a 30-second video feel like a complete story. Many of these creators came up through recipe TikTok and food Instagram, which means they intuitively understand what works in the feed without needing extensive direction. For brands, this means the barrier to high-quality food UGC is lower than it has ever been — you do not need to teach creators how to film food, you just need to match the right creator with the right product and give them a clear brief.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- OUR PROCESS -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:24px">Our <span class="cap v">Process</span></h2>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:20px">

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">01</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Product & Brief</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.5;margin:0">We start by learning everything about your product — ingredients, flavor profile, target audience, key messaging pillars, and any dietary or health claims that need to be highlighted or carefully avoided. We review your existing content, competitor ads, and customer reviews to identify what angles resonate and which gaps to exploit. The brief we build becomes the creative foundation for every piece of content in the campaign.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">02</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Creator Matching</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.5;margin:0">We match your brand with the right creators from our roster of 600+ vetted creators — food bloggers, home cooks, fitness-focused meal preppers, busy parents, or lifestyle creators who match your target demographic and brand aesthetic. We consider factors like kitchen setup, filming quality, on-camera energy, dietary alignment (vegan, keto, gluten-free), and cultural background to ensure the content feels authentic to both the creator and your audience.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">03</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Production & Variants</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.5;margin:0">Creators film based on approved concepts and recipes. We handle recipe development when needed, ensuring dishes are simple enough to execute well on camera but visually compelling. Every video gets 3-5 hook variants for A/B testing, and we produce platform-optimized cuts — 15-second, 30-second, and 60-second versions for TikTok, Instagram Reels, Meta ads, and YouTube Shorts. Captions, CTAs, and music are added in post-production.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">04</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Delivery & Iteration</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.5;margin:0">You receive both raw footage and fully edited videos, organized for easy integration into your content calendar and ad manager. We include performance recommendations — which hooks to test first, which audiences to target, and how to structure your ad sets for efficient testing. After the first round of results, we analyze what worked and iterate on the next batch, doubling down on winning creators, formats, and angles while cutting what underperformed.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- STATS ROW -->
    <section style="padding:48px 24px">
      <div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;background:var(--stage,#111);border-radius:16px;padding:48px 32px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:0;text-align:center">
        <div style="padding:16px;border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1)">
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:700;color:var(--dew,#c1f06b);line-height:1">70+</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e;margin-top:8px;display:block">Food & bev brands</span>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:16px;border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1)">
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:700;color:var(--dew,#c1f06b);line-height:1">600+</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e;margin-top:8px;display:block">Vetted creators</span>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:16px;border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1)">
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:700;color:var(--dew,#c1f06b);line-height:1">3–5</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e;margin-top:8px;display:block">Hook variants per video</span>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:16px">
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:700;color:var(--dew,#c1f06b);line-height:1">5-day</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e;margin-top:8px;display:block">Average delivery</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <style>
        @media(max-width:600px){
          .okh [style*="grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr)"] {
            grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr !important;
          }
          .okh [style*="grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr)"] > div:nth-child(2) {
            border-right: none !important;
          }
          .okh [style*="grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr)"] > div:nth-child(-n+2) {
            border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);
          }
        }
      </style>
    </section>

    <!-- TIPS FOR FOOD & BEVERAGE UGC -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:24px">Tips for Food & Beverage <span class="cap d">UGC</span></h2>

      <div style="display:grid;gap:24px;max-width:64ch">

        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;color:#111">1. Nail the Lighting in Home Kitchens</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.65;margin:0">The single biggest difference between appetizing and unappetizing food video is lighting. Natural window light is your best friend — position the cooking surface near a window and shoot during daylight hours. Avoid overhead fluorescent lighting at all costs, as it casts flat, greenish tones that make food look institutional. If your creators film in the evening, a simple ring light or LED panel positioned at a 45-degree angle can replicate the warmth of natural light. Brief your creators specifically on this: tell them the time of day to film and which direction to face. A well-lit $200 kitchen video will outperform a poorly lit $5,000 studio shoot every time.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;color:#111">2. Make Food Look Appetizing Without a Stylist</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.65;margin:0">Professional food styling uses tricks that do not translate to UGC — hairspray on fruit, motor oil as syrup, cardboard inserts in burgers. You do not need any of that. Instead, focus on a few high-impact basics: wipe the rim of the plate or bowl before filming the final shot, use a contrasting background (white plate on a dark surface or vice versa), and add a "hero garnish" — a sprig of herbs, a drizzle of sauce, a sprinkle of flaky salt — right before filming. The most appetizing food videos focus on movement: pouring, drizzling, breaking, pulling apart, steam rising. Action shots trigger cravings far more than static frames. Brief your creators to capture at least 2-3 "money shots" of the food in motion.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;color:#111">3. Handle FDA and Labeling Compliance Carefully</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.65;margin:0">Food and beverage UGC has regulatory considerations that other categories do not. If your product makes health claims (high protein, low sugar, organic, non-GMO), your creators need to reference them accurately and cannot embellish. The FTC requires clear disclosure of paid partnerships, and the FDA has specific rules about health claims in advertising. Build a compliance checklist into your creative brief: list approved claims word-for-word, flag phrases creators must avoid (like "cures," "treats," or unsubstantiated health benefits), and require that product labels are visible and readable at least once in the video. One compliance violation can cost far more than the entire content budget.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;color:#111">4. Post Food Content at Peak Craving Times</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.65;margin:0">Timing matters more for food content than almost any other category. Post breakfast content between 7-9am, lunch content between 11am-1pm, and dinner or snack content between 4-7pm in your target audience's time zone. Late-night content (9-11pm) works surprisingly well for indulgent snacks and desserts when willpower is low. For paid ads, schedule your campaigns to align with meal-adjacent times for noticeably higher CTR. Our data shows that food ads served during meal-consideration windows (the 1-2 hours before a typical mealtime) see 15-25% higher engagement than the same ads served at random times throughout the day.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;color:#111">5. Brief Creators on Recipes with Precision</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.65;margin:0">Do not send a creator your product and say "make something with it." That approach leads to boring, generic content. Instead, develop 2-3 specific recipe concepts with exact ingredients, rough preparation steps, and a clear vision for the final dish. Include reference videos that show the energy and pacing you want. Specify how prominently the product should appear — is it the star of the dish, or one of several ingredients? Should the creator mention the brand name once, twice, or just show the package? The more specific your recipe brief, the less revision rounds you will need. Leave room for the creator's personality but not for the recipe structure itself.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;color:#111">6. Handle Allergen Mentions Proactively</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#4d4d49;line-height:1.65;margin:0">If your product is free from common allergens (gluten-free, nut-free, dairy-free), that is a selling point that should be mentioned naturally in the content — but it must be accurate. If your product is manufactured in a facility that also processes allergens, creators cannot claim it is allergen-free without the proper qualification. Build allergen talking points into your brief, including the exact phrasing your legal and regulatory teams have approved. On the flip side, if your product contains common allergens, avoid having creators make broad claims about who the product is "perfect for" — a commenter with an allergy flagging a misleading claim can turn a performing ad into a PR issue. The safest approach is to stick to the claims on your label, verbatim.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="faq" style="max-width:700px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:24px">Frequently Asked <span class="cap g">Questions</span></h2>

      <details>
        <summary>Do you ship our product to creators?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>We coordinate the entire product shipping process. Once you approve the creator roster, we provide you with their shipping addresses and you send the product directly. For brands that prefer a hands-off approach, we can also receive a bulk shipment at our fulfillment partner's facility and distribute to individual creators from there. We recommend sending 2-3 units per creator so they have backup for reshoots and can film multiple formats without running out of product. For larger campaigns with 10+ creators, we usually set up a shared spreadsheet to track shipments, delivery confirmation, and filming schedules so nothing falls through the cracks.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How do you handle perishable products?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>Perishable items require extra planning, and we have built a reliable process for it. We coordinate shipping windows with creators to ensure they receive the product fresh and film within 24-48 hours of delivery. For refrigerated or frozen products, we work with temperature-controlled shipping options and schedule deliveries for Monday through Wednesday to avoid weekend warehouse delays. Creators are briefed to film the unboxing immediately upon arrival so the product looks its best on camera. If a product arrives damaged or spoiled, we arrange a replacement shipment right away rather than risking subpar content. We have successfully managed campaigns for ice cream, fresh juice, meal kits, chilled sauces, and frozen snacks — perishability is a logistics challenge, not a dealbreaker.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How much does food UGC cost?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>Our pricing depends on the number of videos, the content format, and whether you need recipe development. A typical food brand campaign starts at $500 for 10 videos with a single creator, including scripting, filming direction, and editing with captions and hooks. Recipe integration content and multi-creator campaigns are priced based on scope — a 30-video campaign across 5 creators with recipe development typically runs $2,000-$4,000. Every package includes multiple hook variants per video, platform-optimized cuts, and usage rights for paid advertising. We price transparently — no hidden fees for revisions, music licensing, or raw footage. Contact us for a custom quote based on your specific product and campaign goals.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What content usage rights do we get?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>All content produced through OKAD comes with full commercial usage rights for paid advertising across all platforms — Meta, TikTok, YouTube, Pinterest, Google, and programmatic. You own the edited content and can use it in perpetuity without additional licensing fees. This includes running the content as ads from your brand's own ad accounts, repurposing clips for your website and email marketing, and editing the footage into new variations. If you want to run the content as whitelisted or partnership ads through the creator's own account, we can arrange that as an add-on. Creator likeness rights are included for the duration specified in your agreement, which we default to 12 months with an option to extend.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can we approve recipes before filming?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>Absolutely — recipe approval is a standard part of our process for food brands. After the brief and creator matching phase, we develop recipe concepts and present them to you for review before any filming begins. You see the recipe, the ingredient list, the visual direction, and the talking points the creator will use. Once you approve, the creator films. For brands with strict dietary messaging (keto, vegan, allergen-free), we also run the recipe and script through a compliance check to ensure every claim is accurate and properly qualified. Most brands do one round of recipe approval and one round of video review before final delivery. Revision requests on the recipe stage are unlimited — we would rather get it right in the concept phase than reshoot.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

